<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/inspection-clipboard.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/inspection-clipboard-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/inspection-clipboard.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Process map</span></nav><p class="tag">Process guide</p><h1>The Insurance Appraisal Process in Hawaii, Mapped</h1><p class="lede">8 stages, from the first disagreement to a signed award — what happens, who does what and where island claims most often stall.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn sun" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn line-light" href="/contact-hawaii-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>Stages</dt><dd>8</dd></div><div><dt>Panel</dt><dd>2 appraisers + umpire</dd></div><div><dt>Binding</dt><dd>2 of 3 signatures</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><ol class="roadmap"><li><a href="#step-1"><span>1</span><b>Pin down the disagreement</b><small>Confirm the claim is accepted in part and write down exactly which items and dollar amounts you dispute.</small></a></li><li><a href="#step-2"><span>2</span><b>Written demand</b><small>Either side invokes the appraisal clause in writing, citing the policy and claim and stating the amount is in dispute.</small></a></li><li><a href="#step-3"><span>3</span><b>Each side names an appraiser</b><small>You and the insurer each choose a competent, disinterested appraiser within the window your policy sets.</small></a></li><li><a href="#step-4"><span>4</span><b>Selecting the umpire</b><small>The two appraisers agree on a neutral umpire, or a court appoints one if they cannot agree in time.</small></a></li><li><a href="#step-5"><span>5</span><b>Inspection and independent scope</b><small>Each appraiser inspects the property and builds an independent scope and price for the covered damage.</small></a></li><li><a href="#step-6"><span>6</span><b>Appraiser-to-appraiser negotiation</b><small>The appraisers compare estimates line by line and agree on as many items as the evidence supports.</small></a></li><li><a href="#step-7"><span>7</span><b>Umpire hearing or review</b><small>Items still in dispute go to the umpire, who reviews both positions and may inspect or hold an informal hearing.</small></a></li><li><a href="#step-8"><span>8</span><b>Signed award and payment</b><small>An award signed by two of the three panel members sets the amount of loss, and the insurer pays under the policy.</small></a></li></ol>
<h2>How insurance appraisal works in Hawaii</h2>
<p>Most homeowners, condo and commercial property policies sold in Hawaii contain an appraisal clause – a short paragraph that lets you and your insurer resolve a disagreement over the <strong>amount</strong> of a covered loss without going to court. It is not a lawsuit and it is not a complaint to the regulator. It is a valuation process run by three people: one appraiser for each side and a neutral umpire.</p>
<p>The steps below follow the order most policies set out. Your policy’s exact wording controls, so read it alongside this guide, and keep in mind that islands add their own wrinkles – inter-island travel, freight-heavy pricing and association-managed buildings all shape how an appraisal runs here.</p>

<h2 id="step-1">Step 1: Pin down the disagreement</h2>
<p>Appraisal is for claims the insurer has accepted at least in part. Before you invoke it, get clear on what you are actually disputing. Compare the carrier’s estimate with your contractor’s bid and list the gaps:</p>
<ul>
<li>Items the carrier left out entirely, such as a second roof slope, interior ceiling repairs or debris haul-off.</li>
<li>Quantities that do not match field measurements.</li>
<li>Unit prices that look like mainland pricing rather than island pricing, including missing freight or barge charges.</li>
<li>General excise tax (GET), overhead and profit, or permit costs that were omitted or applied inconsistently.</li>
</ul>
<p>Send that list to the adjuster first. A well-documented supplement request settles many disputes without appraisal. If you are an AOAO board or a unit owner, gather the association’s file too: the master policy, the reserve study, any engineer’s report and the managing agent’s correspondence.</p>

<h2 id="step-2">Step 2: Written demand</h2>
<p>If the gap remains, either party may demand appraisal in writing. A clear demand typically identifies the policy and claim numbers, states that the parties disagree on the amount of loss, invokes the appraisal provision and names your appraiser or commits to naming one within the policy’s window. Send it in a way you can prove, such as certified mail plus email.</p>
<p>Check whether your policy requires a sworn proof of loss, an examination under oath or other steps first. If a claim has been denied outright, or a suit deadline is getting close, speak with a Hawaii attorney before relying on appraisal.</p>

<h2 id="step-3">Step 3: Each side names an appraiser</h2>
<p>Each party picks a competent and disinterested appraiser. Many policies give each side about 20 days from the demand, but windows vary, so calendar yours. Your appraiser should understand Hawaii construction – single-wall and plantation-style homes, post-and-pier foundations, corrugated metal and hip roofs, high-rise concrete towers – and the pricing realities of the island where the property sits.</p>
<p>Ask any appraiser, including us, to put the fee arrangement in writing before you sign. Some policies or courts look closely at contingency fees when judging whether an appraiser is disinterested.</p>

<h2 id="step-4">Step 4: Selecting the umpire</h2>
<p>The two appraisers then try to agree on a neutral umpire. It is common to settle on one early, so the panel is complete if a dispute stalls. If they cannot agree within the policy’s time limit, policies commonly allow either party to ask a court of record to appoint one; in Hawaii that usually means the circuit court where the property is located.</p>
<p>Island logistics matter here. An umpire based on Oahu may need to fly to Kauai, Maui or the Big Island to see a property, and travel costs are often shared. Some panels choose an umpire on the same island; others accept remote review of photos and measurements for smaller disputes.</p>

<h2 id="step-5">Step 5: Inspection and independent scope</h2>
<p>Each appraiser inspects the property and builds an independent estimate. On the neighbor islands this often means coordinating flights, rental cars and access to gated or remote properties in Puna, Upcountry Maui or Kauai’s north shore. For a condo tower, it may mean scheduling with the managing agent, building engineer and several unit owners at once.</p>
<p>A strong scope documents measurements, photos, moisture readings and material samples, and prices the work the way it would actually be done on that island: freight to the job, inter-island barge where needed, local labor availability and GET applied consistently.</p>

<h2 id="step-6">Step 6: Appraiser-to-appraiser negotiation</h2>
<p>Most of the work happens here. The appraisers exchange estimates, walk the property together if useful and work through the differences line by line. They often agree on measurements quickly and spend more time on pricing, matching, code upgrades and whether certain damage is part of the loss being valued.</p>
<p>Many appraisals end at this stage, with both appraisers signing an agreed award and no umpire decision needed.</p>

<h2 id="step-7">Step 7: Umpire hearing or review</h2>
<p>Items the appraisers cannot resolve go to the umpire. Each appraiser submits a written position with supporting evidence. Depending on the umpire and the size of the dispute, the review may be a paper review, a site visit or an informal hearing – sometimes held by video when panel members are on different islands. The umpire decides each disputed item, not the claim as a whole.</p>

<h2 id="step-8">Step 8: Signed award and payment</h2>
<p>An award signed by any two of the three panel members sets the amount of loss. A well-drafted award separates replacement cost value, depreciation and actual cash value, and breaks figures out by coverage – dwelling, other structures, contents and loss of use. The insurer then pays according to the policy, less the deductible and prior payments. Recoverable depreciation is commonly released once repairs are complete, so keep invoices and photos of the finished work.</p>

<h2>Timing and costs</h2>
<p>There is no fixed timetable. A straightforward residential roof or water dispute is often resolved within a few months; large condo, hurricane or wildfire claims commonly take longer, especially when inspections must be scheduled across islands or materials pricing is in flux after a regional event.</p>
<p>Each side usually pays its own appraiser, and umpire fees are commonly split equally. Inter-island travel, engineer reports and building access fees can add to cost on the neighbor islands. Our <a href="/island-claim-tools/claim-gap-fee-estimator/">claim gap fee estimator</a> can help you judge whether the gap justifies the expense, and the <a href="/island-claim-tools/claim-timeline-builder/">claim timeline builder</a> helps you track deadlines.</p>
<p>If you have questions about how your insurer is handling the claim generally, the Insurance Division of the Hawaii Department of Commerce and Consumer Affairs accepts consumer inquiries and complaints. That is separate from appraisal and can run alongside it.</p>

<div class="note"><b class="h">What appraisal does not decide</b>Appraisal sets the amount of loss. It generally does not decide whether a loss is covered, whether an exclusion applies or whether the insurer acted in bad faith – those are legal questions for a Hawaii attorney. Also, demanding appraisal does not necessarily pause your policy’s suit-limitation deadline. If that deadline is approaching, talk with an attorney promptly.</div>
